# Shared scratch cleanup failure contract

Assigned private issue 224, rooted at
`7f88f3fe1ab8ee6c7279fa8b64dce7de5afac453`. Related fixture-specific issue 139
and its separate ownership/evidence remain intact. Root alone integrates.

Required outcome: failures removing owned test scratch must reach the owning
test/controller with their original cause. A directory remains registered
until absence is confirmed; other registered cleanup may continue, but failed
paths remain unresolved. No detached unobserved cleaner or new clock trigger
is permitted. Explicit debug retention remains distinct from removal. Normal
owned teardown must close its native resource and confirm removal.

Scope: actual project helper, focused source-qualified tests and evidence.
No existing scratch is selected, no unrelated process is controlled, and no
global deletion/approval policy, consumer database or external service changes.
The retained checkout's helper, declaration, existing tests, setup and purge
support match the current root preimages exactly; no engine source is loaded
for this helper-only verification.

## Reproduction and source correction

The complete initial failure-control file ran on the actual root-preimage
helper: two cases passed and four failed, native exit one. A controlled
filesystem removal error disappeared from removeScratchDir. Aggregate cleanup
returned success, dropped the failed registration and attempted detached spawn;
the fixture intercepted and refused that spawn before any process launched.
Its launch error was swallowed too. Exit cleanup produced neither failure
diagnostics nor a failing success status. All original red reports and child
stdout/stderr remain under registered ignored
`.runtime/scratch-cleanup-224-20261031/red/`.

The helper now propagates single-removal errors unchanged. Aggregate cleanup
attempts each owned path, removes only confirmed absences from registration,
and throws AggregateError containing every original failure and unresolved
path. The exit handler reports that same failure and changes a zero/undefined
exit status to one, preserving a prior nonzero code and its diagnostics. No
child process is imported/launched and the delayed cleaner is removed. Existing
bounded native rmSync retry settings are unchanged; no timer/schedule was added.

Removal checks actual presence with lstat: only ENOENT confirms absence;
unreadable status remains an error. A successful rmSync that leaves a directory
present is not success. An exception remains observable even if removal happened
before it; a later explicit check may confirm absence without replaying removal.
Existing paths must be registered to this process, remain directories rather
than replacement links, and still carry this checkout/machine's manifest.
An unknown/changed/unreadable owner is refused, not deleted. Explicit debug keep
leaves both the directory and its registration intact. Public declarations and
ordinary callers retain the existing void-returning API.

